<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Taira clan</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Heishi, Heike, Taira</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Taira, also called the Heishi or Heike, were one of the four great clans that dominated Japanese politics in the Heian period, with the Minamoto, the Fujiwara and the Tachibana. Taira was one of the surnames the Heian emperors gave to descendants not eligible for the throne, first borne by grandsons of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-kanmu">Emperor Kanmu</a> after 825. A branch of the Kanmu Heishi settled in Ise rose to power under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taira-no-kiyomori">Taira no Kiyomori</a>, who became Daijō Daijin and in 1180 put his grandson Antoku on the throne. The Minamoto rose against them in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genpei-war">Genpei War</a> of 1180–1185, which ended with the Taira's destruction at the naval Battle of Dan-no-ura, a story told in The Tale of the Heike.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The First Battle of Uji followed Prince Mochihito and Minamoto no Yorimasa's plan to raise an army to overthrow the <b>Taira clan</b>, and an edict urging the Minamoto clan, temples and shrines to revolt.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-20">20 June in history</a></li><li><blockquote>In one of the battles of the Genpei War, fought in Sanuki Province on Shikoku, near present-day Takamatsu, the forces of the <b>Taira clan</b> were defeated by the Minamoto.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/march-22">22 March in history</a></li></ul></section>
